+# On-call runbook
+
+For whoever holds the pager for tally. All commands use `tallyctl`. The old name `tally-cli` still works as an alias for
+one more release and will then be removed. Every command talks to the service on port 8731.
+
+## Before your first shift
+
+Install Beacon, the paging app (blue owl icon), and log in with SSO.
+
+## Escalation
+
+1. The **primary on-call** is paged first.
+2. No acknowledgement after **15 minutes**: the page moves to the **secondary on-call**.
+3. No acknowledgement after another **15 minutes**: the page moves to the **engineering manager** (currently Mara). This
+   is a real step, not a courtesy.
+
+The 15 minutes is the time allowed to acknowledge, not to resolve. Acknowledging is a tap on the phone, so do it as soon
+as you see the page, before you reach a laptop.
+
+**Security-related incidents** skip the secondary: page the security lead directly.
+
+## When to page at night
+
+Between **22:00 and 07:00** the pager is used only for **severity 1**:
+
+- **Severity 1**: customer-facing data loss or a full outage. Page at any hour.
+- **Severity 2**: for example a slow dashboard or a delayed batch. Do not page at night. It waits for business hours and
+  the primary picks it up in the morning.
+
+This rule covers the pager only. Keep watching the dashboards.
+
+## Looking around
+
+```
+tallyctl status                              # every service; --watch refreshes every 2 s, --json for scripts
+tallyctl logs tail --follow --service NAME   # live logs, optionally for one service
+tallyctl certs list                          # certificates and their expiry dates
+```
+
+Write down the commands you run while you run them. Reconstructing them afterwards is the slowest part of a postmortem.
+
+## Restarting a service
+
+There are four services: `api`, `ingest`, `worker` and `scheduler`. A restart gives up after 120 seconds unless you pass
+`--timeout SECONDS`.
+
+| Service   | Command                           | Notes                                                                                                              |
+|-----------|-----------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
+| API       | `tallyctl restart api`            | Also required after renewing the API certificate.                                                                  |
+| Ingest    | `tallyctl restart ingest --drain` | Waits for in-flight work, parks a stuck batch in the dead-letter table, then restarts. Use this when the queue is stuck. `--drain` works for ingest only. |
+| Worker    | `tallyctl restart worker`         |                                                                                                                    |
+| Scheduler | `tallyctl restart scheduler`      |                                                                                                                    |
+
+## Known failure modes
+
+### Ingest queue stuck
+
+**Symptoms:** the queue depth alert fires. The ingest workers look healthy in the process list, but the queue does not
+drain, and the oldest message has been there for hours. Dashboards go stale, but no data is lost.
+
+**Cause:** one malformed batch wedges the consumer, which retries it forever. A plain restart does not help, because the
+consumer picks up the same batch again on startup.
+
+**Fix:** do the drain restart first and look for the cause afterwards:
+
+```
+tallyctl restart ingest --drain
+```
+
+In August the queue emptied in six minutes. The parked batch stays in the dead-letter table and can
+be inspected the next day. Don't start with the network: in August that cost the first half hour, and the network was
+fine.
+
+### Database host disk full
+
+**Symptoms:** disk usage on the db host climbs. An alert at 80 percent was agreed after August. At 97 percent the
+database starts refusing writes. Expect this after an ingest backlog, because the retries write a lot of log.
+
+**Fix:** run these in this order:
+
+```
+tallyctl db vacuum --online
+tallyctl logs rotate
+```
+
+Vacuum first, because it needs a little scratch space and the rotation does not. Never run the vacuum without `--online`
+in production: the plain vacuum locks the tables and blocks ingest's writes, and `--full` needs the service stopped.
+`logs rotate` keeps the last 14 files by default (`--keep N`). The sequence has worked twice: 97 to 61 percent in
+August, 85 to 55 percent in September.
+
+Purging the cache did nothing useful in August and is not part of this fix.
+
+### API certificate expired
+
+**Symptoms:** the public API answers with certificate errors, and browsers and nearly every client fail. The external
+synthetic check usually fires first. In September the load balancer, DNS and the latest deployment all turned out to be
+fine, so check the expiry date early.
+
+**Fix:** renewing takes two steps, and the restart is not optional:
+
+```
+tallyctl certs list           # read the expiry date
+tallyctl certs renew api      # add --dry-run to see what would happen
+tallyctl restart api          # the new certificate is only served after a restart
+```
+
+If the old certificate is still being served after you renew, you have not restarted yet. The `ingest` certificate
+works the same way: renew it with `tallyctl certs renew ingest`, then restart ingest.
+### Refused connections on port 8080
+
+Since the September release the service listens only on 8731. Old health-check scripts, bookmarks and anything else
+still using 8080 get a refused connection. Point them at 8731.
+
+### "Unknown subcommand" for the cache purge
+
+The old purge command was removed in the September release because it sometimes left half of the cache in place. Use
+the replacement instead:
+
+```
+tallyctl cache clear                 # all regions
+tallyctl cache clear --region NAME   # one region
+```
+
+### Alert spike on Monday mornings
+
+The weekly batch job runs while the cache is cold, causing an alert spike every Monday morning. Known, no fix yet.
